To set up file permissions I created users with useradd and groups with groupadd,
then added users to groups with usermod -aG groupname username.
I changed who owns a file or folder with chown user:group path
, which sets both the owner and the group. 
Permissions are set with chmod using three numbers for user, group, and others,
where read is 4, write is 2, and execute is 1, so 7 is full access, 6 is read and write,
5 is read and execute, 4 is read only, and 0 is nothing. I set both directories to 750 so the
owner has full access, the group can open and list the folder, and everyone else is blocked.
I set both files to 640 so only the owner can edit, the group can read, and others get nothing.
You can check permissions with ls -l for files and ls -ld for the directory itself.
